"Where does the Sun rise? Most people say \"in the east,\" and that is almost right. But if you watch the sunrise from the same window every week and compare it with a landmark, such as a tall building or a mountain, you will notice something surprising. The Sun comes up in a slightly different direction each day. In summer it rises north of east, and in winter it rises south of east.": "fab0444d000f",
"Where does the Sun rise? Most people say \"in the east,\" and that is almost right. But if you watch the sunrise from the same window every week and compare it with a landmark, such as a tall building or a mountain, you will notice something surprising. The Sun comes up in a slightly different direction each day. In summer it rises north of east, and in winter it rises south of east. In Changhua, the sunrise point swings to about 26 degrees north of east in June and about 25 degrees south of east in December. That is more than 50 degrees along the horizon, the width of five fists held at arm's length. The Sun rises due east only around the spring and fall equinoxes, in March and September. The sunset point swings the same way in the west. Why does this happen? Earth's axis is tilted by about 23.5 degrees. As Earth travels around the Sun, the Sun's daily path across our sky shifts north for half the year and south for the other half. Each day the Sun follows a circle across the sky, and the place where that circle meets the horizon is where the Sun rises. When the whole circle moves north, the sunrise point moves north too. The sunrise point does not move at the same speed all year. Near the equinoxes, it moves almost one Sun-width every day. Near the solstices, it slows down, seems to stand still for several days, and then turns around. The word solstice comes from Latin words meaning \"the Sun stands still.\" The Chinese names, 夏至 and 冬至, mean that summer and winter have reached their limit. Ancient people noticed this swing and used the horizon as a calendar. At Taosi in Shanxi, China, archaeologists found the remains of an observatory more than 4,000 years old: a half circle of thirteen rammed-earth pillars. Standing at the center, an observer could watch the Sun rise through the gaps between the pillars. On the winter solstice it rose in the second gap, and on the summer solstice in the twelfth. Knowing the date helped people decide when to plant and when to harvest. You can see the swing in a city, too. When a long, straight street points to where the Sun sets, the Sun can hang right at the end of the street between the buildings. In New York this is called Manhattanhenge, and in Taiwan it is called 懸日, the \"hanging Sun.\" Because the sunset point passes each direction twice a year, once going and once coming back, there are two henge seasons. Look below for today's sunrise in Changhua and a finder that tells you when the Sun will line up with a street.": "8c783378502c",
